\f0\b 1. Human oversight\

\f1\b0 \
I used the skill as decision support and reviewed its data summaries, modeling choices, and final results. I added a checkpoint after data understanding so I could correct the skill's interpretation before modeling. I also added input validation so it could identify file problems and ask for clarification when the train, validation, and test roles were unclear.\
\
An important change was moving from trying several model classes at once to a baseline-first workflow. I changed the skill to fit and assess Logistic Regression (or any baseline model that fits the task) first, inspect where it made errors, and only then test HistGradientBoosting when the baseline results suggested that nonlinear effects or interactions might help. This made the model comparison easier to interpret and kept added complexity tied to evidence. It is the workflow described in the final report.\
\
Earlier versions paused after every workflow step. I found that this added interaction and token use without helping me complete a run, so I reduced the routine process to one checkpoint and a summary of the main decisions. I also explored a workflow diagram, an educational mode, and a closer match to the report template. I dropped those additions when they increased complexity or caused performance problems. I used Gemini alongside my Colab notebook to help explain mathematical concepts and code syntax. This helped me understand parts of the workflow, but it was not independent verification.\
\
If I ran the project again, I would define the core requirements earlier and test one complete run before adding optional features. I would also reserve space for a compact table of per-class precision and recall instead of presenting those results only in prose.\
\

\f0\b 2. Critical evaluation\

\f1\b0 \
I agree with using average precision (AP) as the primary metric because the "yes" class is the minority and AP evaluates how well the model ranks positive cases. I would explain it alongside the confusion matrix, precision, and recall, which are easier to interpret at the report's fixed 0.5 threshold. Accuracy alone would be misleading because always predicting "no" would achieve about 76% accuracy.\
\
I questioned the rare-category pooling described in preprocessing. The report says levels with fewer than 20 rows were pooled, but the examples it gives do not show that any level met this cutoff. My check found that none did. If that check is correct, the report should say that the pooling rule had no effect on this dataset rather than suggest it reduced coefficient instability here.\
\
